{"meta":{"course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"08","chapter_title":"Online &amp; Distance Learning","title":"Module 8: Online &amp; Distance Learning Design","description":"Design defensible online courses using Moore's three types of interaction and the Community of Inquiry model, and choose modes (asynchronous, synchronous, hybrid) deliberately.","learning_objectives":["Allocate Moore's three interaction types (learner-content, learner-instructor, learner-learner) intentionally across an online course.","Apply the Community of Inquiry model (cognitive, social, teaching presence) to diagnose deep-learning gaps.","Match mode (asynchronous, synchronous, hybrid) to objective rather than treating mode as a default."],"concept_ids":["online_learning_design","moore_three_interactions","community_of_inquiry","cognitive_presence","social_presence","teaching_presence"],"prereq_section_ids":["inst-design/02/07"],"practice_ids":["online_moore_balance","online_coi_phases","online_async_vs_sync","online_teaching_presence"],"estimated_time_minutes":13,"order":8},"content":"# Designing for Online Learning\n\n> **\"Distance is a pedagogical, not a geographic, phenomenon.\"**\n> - Michael G. Moore, on transactional distance\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Explain** why an online course is not a recorded version of a classroom course\n2. **Classify** course activities using Moore's three types of interaction\n3. **Diagnose** an online course with the Community of Inquiry (CoI) model's three presences\n4. **Select** a delivery mode (asynchronous, synchronous, hybrid) that fits a stated objective\n5. **Apply** these frameworks to redesign a weak online course, step by step\n\n---\n\n## Opening: A Course That Ran but Did Not Teach\n\nA graduate program moved its flagship 12-week seminar online over a single summer. The instructor did the obvious thing: recorded each 90-minute lecture, posted the videos, and required three discussion-forum posts per week graded only on length. Completion rates held up. Yet the end-of-term transfer task, the same one used in the in-person version, showed a sharp drop. Learners could recite the readings but could not use them on a novel case.\n\nNothing was technically broken. The videos played, the forum filled with posts, grades were entered on time. The course failed in a quieter way: it reproduced the *delivery* of a classroom while silently dropping the *interactions* that made the classroom work. This section gives you the diagnostic vocabulary to see that failure before launch, not after.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Online learning is not classroom learning sent through a different pipe. It is a distinct design problem with its own theory base, its own failure modes, and its own quality criteria.\n\n---\n\n## Why Online Learning Is Not Just Classroom Learning Recorded\n\nThe most expensive mistake in online instructional design is to treat the digital course as a classroom course delivered through a different pipe. Two reasons that fails. First, the **temporal and spatial structure** of online learning is fundamentally different; learners are not synchronized in time, are not co-located, and are not under the same continuous social pressure that an in-room cohort produces. Second, the **modes of interaction** that the classroom takes for granted (eye contact, hallway questions, the instructor noticing a confused face) disappear unless the design replaces them with something deliberate.\n\nOnline learning design therefore has its own theory base. Two frameworks dominate the literature: **Moore's three types of interaction** and the **Community of Inquiry (CoI) model** by Garrison, Anderson, and Archer. Both are foundational in Reiser and Dempsey, in Smith and Ragan, and in nearly every online-learning chapter of the canonical ID texts.\n\nThis module covers the design moves that distinguish a defensible online course from a recorded lecture series.\n\n---\n\n## Moore's Three Types of Interaction\n\nMichael Moore (1989) argued that any instructional system can be analyzed by which interactions it supports and how strongly. He named three:\n\n**Learner-content interaction.** The learner reads, watches, queries, manipulates, or otherwise engages the subject matter directly. This is the only type that exists in a self-paced, asynchronous, instructor-absent course. Strong learner-content design pays attention to retrieval practice, worked examples, segmenting, and embedded checks (Gagné Event 6 lives here).\n\n**Learner-instructor interaction.** The learner receives direct feedback, coaching, or correction from a content expert. Online forms include graded feedback on submissions, instructor-led webinars, threaded forum responses, and one-to-one coaching messages. The frequency and specificity of this interaction predict perceived course quality more strongly than any other variable in the empirical literature.\n\n**Learner-learner interaction.** Learners exchange ideas, work, or critique with peers. Online forms include forum discussion, peer review, group projects, and synchronous collaborative work. Empirical effects are more variable than the other two types: well-structured peer interaction has strong effects on retention and transfer; loose, unmoderated forums often have negligible effect.\n\nA balanced online course intentionally allocates **all three** types. A course that only has learner-content interaction is a textbook with a video player. A course that only has learner-instructor interaction is a tutoring relationship. A course that only has learner-learner interaction is a discussion club. None of those alone is a course.\n\n### A Diagnostic Table\n\nUse this table to label what each activity in a course actually *is*, and to spot which interaction is missing.\n\n| Interaction Type | Typical Online Form | What It Builds | Failure Symptom When Absent |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Learner-content | Readings, videos, simulations, embedded quizzes | Encoding, retrieval, practice | Course feels like a passive reading list |\n| Learner-instructor | Feedback on work, office hours, expert facilitation | Correction, calibration, motivation | Learners cannot tell whether they are right |\n| Learner-learner | Peer review, structured discussion, group tasks | Elaboration, perspective-taking, transfer | No sense of cohort; ideas never tested socially |\n\n> **Key Insight:** \"Three forum posts a week\" is not automatically learner-learner interaction. If no one responds and no instructor shapes the thread, it is learner-content interaction wearing a costume.\n\nMatch each of Moore's three interaction types to the failure symptom that appears when it is absent. Reading the symptom backward to the missing interaction is the core diagnostic skill.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Learner-content interaction\",\"definition\":\"When absent, the course feels like a passive reading list with no retrieval or practice\"},{\"term\":\"Learner-instructor interaction\",\"definition\":\"When absent, learners cannot tell whether they are right; no correction, calibration, or expert feedback\"},{\"term\":\"Learner-learner interaction\",\"definition\":\"When absent, there is no sense of cohort and ideas are never tested socially against peers\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"An online course consists of recorded videos, auto-graded quizzes, and a discussion board where posts are graded only on word count and nobody is required to reply. By Moore's criteria, how many distinct interaction types does it actually support?\" explanation=\"Only one. Videos and quizzes are learner-content. The length-graded board with no required replies and no instructor shaping is learner-content wearing a costume, not real learner-learner interaction. Learner-instructor and genuine learner-learner interaction are both absent.\" options='[{\"text\":\"One: it collapses to learner-content interaction\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Two: learner-content and learner-learner\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"All three\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## The Community of Inquiry Model\n\nGarrison, Anderson, and Archer's Community of Inquiry framework (2000, refined many times since) describes the conditions under which deep learning emerges in online environments. They name three overlapping presences.\n\n**Cognitive presence** is the extent to which learners construct meaning through sustained reflection and discourse. It moves through four phases: a triggering event raises a question, exploration gathers relevant information, integration synthesizes ideas, and resolution commits to a defensible answer. Online courses fail at cognitive presence when discussion stops at exploration and never reaches integration or resolution.\n\n**Social presence** is the degree to which participants experience each other as real people, not just usernames. It includes affective expression, open communication, and group cohesion. Without social presence, learner-learner interaction becomes transactional and shallow.\n\n**Teaching presence** is the design and direction of the educational experience. It includes course architecture, facilitation of discourse, and direct instruction. Teaching presence is the variable an instructional designer most directly controls; it lives in the syllabus, the prompts, the rubrics, the announcements, and the timing of feedback.\n\nThe intersection of all three is where deep learning happens. A course strong in only one or two presences may feel functional but rarely produces durable transfer.\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"community-of-inquiry\"></concept-diagram>\n\n> **Connecting the Two Frameworks:** The frameworks are not rivals; they layer. Moore tells you *which channels of interaction* exist; CoI tells you *what psychological condition* each channel must produce. Learner-instructor interaction is the main carrier of teaching presence. Learner-learner interaction is where social presence either forms or fails. Cognitive presence cuts across all three channels and is the ultimate target.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Moore and CoI are said to 'layer' rather than compete. Explain the relationship using one concrete activity: a rubric-based peer-review task. Which Moore channel is it, and which CoI presence(s) does that channel carry?\" answer=\"A rubric-based peer review is a learner-learner interaction in Moore's terms (peers critique each other's work). In CoI terms that channel is where social presence forms (learners experience each other as real people whose feedback matters) and it also advances cognitive presence by pushing discourse past mere exploration toward integration, because the rubric forces learners to synthesize criteria against a peer's actual artifact. Moore names the channel; CoI names the psychological condition the channel must produce. The rubric is the teaching-presence lever that makes the learner-learner channel actually generate those conditions.\"></self-explain>\n\n---\n\n## Asynchronous, Synchronous, Hybrid: Choosing the Mode\n\nThe mode question is not \"which is better\" but \"which fits the objective.\"\n\n**Asynchronous** (learners engage on their own time) excels at deep reading, reflective writing, complex problem solving, and learners who need time to formulate. It struggles with real-time skill demonstration, quick clarification, and the social cohesion that synchronous time builds. Defensible asynchronous design uses pacing scaffolds (weekly checkpoints, due dates, peer-review windows) to prevent procrastination and isolation.\n\n**Synchronous** (learners engage at the same time) excels at real-time discourse, demonstration, and rapid feedback loops. It struggles with timezone-distributed cohorts, learners who process slowly under verbal pressure, and content that benefits from rewatching. Defensible synchronous design budgets time deliberately, uses breakout structures rather than long lectures, and records sessions for asynchronous reuse.\n\n**Hybrid / blended** combines the modes intentionally; for example, asynchronous pre-work, a synchronous discourse session, then asynchronous follow-up. The mistake here is to layer modes additively rather than design for their interaction. Each mode should do what it does best; redundancy across modes wastes learner time.\n\n### Mode-Selection Decision Table\n\n| If the objective requires... | Best-fit mode | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Time to formulate, deep reading, written reasoning | Asynchronous | Removes verbal-pressure and timing constraints |\n| Live skill demonstration, rapid back-and-forth | Synchronous | Real-time feedback loop is the point |\n| Cohort cohesion plus deep individual work | Hybrid | Synchronous builds presence; async builds depth |\n| Distributed timezones, large enrollment | Asynchronous (with optional sync) | Scales without excluding learners |\n| High-stakes negotiation, role-play, debate | Synchronous | Co-presence drives authentic interaction |\n\n> **Critical Check:** A synchronous mass lecture is the most expensive mode used for the lowest-value purpose. If the synchronous hour is one-way delivery, the objective belonged in asynchronous video.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example: Redesigning the Failing Seminar\n\nReturn to the seminar from the opening: recorded 90-minute lectures, three length-graded forum posts per week, no synchronous time, weak transfer scores. We will diagnose and redesign it with both frameworks, step by step.\n\n**Step 1: Inventory the interactions (Moore).**\nList every graded or required activity and label its interaction type.\n\n| Activity | Interaction type present |\n| --- | --- |\n| Watch 90-min recorded lecture | Learner-content |\n| Post 3 forum messages, graded on length | Learner-content (no one is required to respond) |\n| End-of-term transfer task | Learner-content |\n\nThe audit is decisive: **all three activities collapse to learner-content.** Learner-instructor and learner-learner interaction are absent. By Moore's criterion this is \"a textbook with a video player,\" which explains the recall-without-transfer result.\n\n**Step 2: Locate the missing presences (CoI).**\nMap the same activities onto the three presences.\n\n- *Teaching presence:* low. There is course architecture, but no facilitation, no feedback, no shaping of discourse.\n- *Social presence:* near zero. Posts are monologues graded by word count; no one experiences anyone else as a real person.\n- *Cognitive presence:* stalls at exploration. Learners post information but never integrate or resolve, because nothing pushes them to.\n\n**Step 3: Diagnose the mode-purpose match.**\nThe objective is transfer to novel cases, which requires elaboration and argument. That objective is poorly served by one-way recorded video alone and would be well served by structured discourse. The current mode allocation is backwards: maximum effort on passive content, zero on the interaction the objective needs.\n\nBefore reading the prescription, work the redesign decisions yourself.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Redesign the failing seminar\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The Moore audit shows all three activities collapse to learner-content. The objective is transfer to novel cases. What does that objective most need?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Structured discourse: elaboration and argument among learners and with an instructor\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Transfer to novel cases is built by elaboration and argument, not by more passive content.\"},{\"label\":\"Higher-resolution lecture videos\",\"next\":\"bad1\",\"feedback\":\"More polished content is still only learner-content; it does not add the missing interactions.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"You replace the length-graded forum. Which replacement restores learner-learner interaction AND pushes cognitive presence past exploration?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Structured peer review where each learner critiques two peers against a rubric\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. The rubric forces integration and seeds social presence.\"},{\"label\":\"Require five forum posts instead of three, still graded on length\",\"next\":\"bad2\",\"feedback\":\"More length-graded posts is the same costume; no discourse is shaped.\"}]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"The objective also needs discourse that reaches RESOLUTION, and the instructor is invisible. What single addition best fixes both?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A fortnightly recorded synchronous case session with breakouts, plus a weekly instructor synthesis post\",\"next\":\"good\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Live breakouts drive resolution and social presence; the synthesis post makes teaching presence visible.\"},{\"label\":\"Convert every lecture to a live mass webinar\",\"next\":\"bad3\",\"feedback\":\"A one-way synchronous lecture is the costliest mode for the lowest-value purpose; it adds no discourse.\"}]},\"good\":{\"prompt\":\"Your redesign restored all three Moore interactions and gave cognitive presence a path from triggering to resolution, by adding SPECIFIC missing elements rather than undirected volume.\",\"choices\":[]},\"bad1\":{\"prompt\":\"Dead end: polishing content does not add the missing interactions. Restart.\",\"choices\":[]},\"bad2\":{\"prompt\":\"Dead end: more length-graded posts is learner-content in costume. Restart.\",\"choices\":[]},\"bad3\":{\"prompt\":\"Dead end: a one-way webinar wastes the most expensive mode. Restart.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n**Step 4: Prescribe targeted changes (not \"more of everything\").**\n\n| Problem found | Targeted fix | Framework restored |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 90-min monologue, no retrieval | Segment into 12-15 min chunks with embedded retrieval checks | Stronger learner-content; cognitive presence triggering/exploration |\n| Length-graded forum | Replace with structured peer review: each learner critiques two peers against a rubric | Learner-learner; social presence; cognitive integration |\n| No instructor contact | Weekly instructor synthesis post naming strong/weak moves, plus rubric-based feedback within 4 days | Learner-instructor; teaching presence |\n| No discourse that reaches resolution | Add a fortnightly 50-min synchronous case session with breakouts, recorded | Cognitive presence resolution; social presence; hybrid mode |\n\n**Step 5: Verify the redesign closes the gap.**\nRe-run the Step 1 audit on the new design: content activities now carry retrieval (content), the rubric-based critique adds learner-learner, and the synthesis post plus timely feedback adds learner-instructor. All three Moore interactions are present and intentionally weighted. Re-run the Step 2 audit: teaching presence is now visible in the synthesis and rubrics, social presence is seeded by required peer critique and the live session, and cognitive presence now has a path from triggering through resolution. The redesign is defensible because each change restores a *specific* missing element rather than adding undirected volume.\n\n> **Think About It:** Notice what we did *not* do: we did not add more videos, raise video resolution, or convert everything to live lectures. Each of those would have been \"more effort, same missing interaction.\"\n\n---\n\n## Designing for Self-Regulation\n\nOnline learners must regulate their own attention, pacing, and persistence to a degree classroom learners rarely do. Three design moves support self-regulation.\n\n**Make pacing visible.** Show the learner where they are in the course, what is upcoming, and how their pace compares to expected pace. Hidden progress correlates with disengagement.\n\n**Build in low-stakes checkpoints.** Frequent, short formative checks anchor attention and create natural breaks. They also generate the data the adaptive system needs.\n\n**Surface learning strategies, not just content.** Tell the learner *how* to study the material, which prompts to retrieve, which mistakes to expect. Self-regulated learners exist on a spectrum, and the design should not assume the strong end.\n\n---\n\n## Common Online-Learning Design Anti-Patterns\n\nA short audit of frequent mistakes:\n\n- **Recorded-lecture saturation.** Long uninterrupted videos with no embedded checks. Working memory fades within minutes; the learner is not retrieving.\n- **Forum-as-grading.** Requiring forum posts purely for participation credit, with no teaching presence directing the discourse. Cognitive presence stays at exploration and never integrates.\n- **Feedback latency.** Submission turnarounds longer than a week destroy formative value. Feedback that arrives after the learner has moved on functions as a grade, not a learning signal.\n- **Tool sprawl.** Different modules using different tools, each with its own login. Cognitive load from interface variability becomes a hidden tax on learning.\n- **Synchronous mass lectures.** Treating webinars as broadcasts. The synchronous mode wasted on one-way content delivery is the most expensive mode for the lowest-value purpose.\n\nEach of these signals a missing presence, a mode-purpose mismatch, or a self-regulation gap.\n\n---\n\n## Quality Audit for an Online Course\n\nBefore launch:\n\n- Does each module include all three Moore interactions, weighted intentionally?\n- Does the course architecture make teaching presence visible (clear sequencing, rubrics, expectations)?\n- Are cognitive presence prompts designed to push past exploration into integration and resolution?\n- Are social presence opportunities built in (introductions, low-stakes peer interactions, named instructor voice)?\n- Is mode (asynchronous/synchronous/hybrid) chosen per objective rather than as a default?\n- Are pacing, formative checkpoints, and learning-strategy guidance explicit?\n\nIf several answers are \"no,\" the course will run, but it will under-perform its potential.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Online Learning Design\" context=\"A 12-week graduate seminar moves online. The instructor records 90-minute lectures, requires three forum posts per week graded only on length, and offers no synchronous time. Diagnose using Moore's three interactions and the Community of Inquiry model.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"online_learning_design\",\"moore_three_interactions\",\"community_of_inquiry\",\"cognitive_presence\",\"social_presence\",\"teaching_presence\"]'></concept-map>\n\n### Key Frameworks\n\n| Framework | Core elements | Designer's question |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Moore's interactions | Learner-content, learner-instructor, learner-learner | Which interaction channels exist, and how are they weighted? |\n| Community of Inquiry | Cognitive, social, teaching presence | What psychological condition does each channel produce? |\n| Mode selection | Asynchronous, synchronous, hybrid | Which mode fits this specific objective? |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- An online course is a distinct design problem, not a recorded classroom.\n- A defensible course intentionally allocates all three Moore interactions; only learner-content present is the classic failure pattern.\n- The three CoI presences must intersect for deep learning; teaching presence is the lever the designer most directly controls.\n- Choose mode by objective, not by default; a one-way synchronous lecture is the costliest mode for the lowest-value purpose.\n- Redesign by restoring the *specific* missing element, not by adding undirected volume.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn the next section we extend this interaction-and-presence lens to **assessment and feedback at a distance**, where teaching presence becomes concrete in rubrics, turnaround times, and the formative-versus-summative balance that online courses so often get wrong.\n\n---\n\n### Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"An online module consists entirely of recorded videos and end-of-module multiple-choice quizzes. Which intervention would most defensibly improve it?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Add more videos at higher resolution.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Layer in deliberate learner-instructor interaction (timely, specific feedback) and structured learner-learner interaction (peer review or discussion that pushes past exploration into integration), so all three Moore interactions are present.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Switch every video to live synchronous lectures.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. The course currently has only learner-content interaction. Adding intentional learner-instructor and learner-learner interaction is the canonical fix; switching everything to synchronous would not address the missing structured discourse.\"></interactive-quiz>\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"online_moore_balance","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"moore_three_interactions","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["moore_three_interactions","online_learning_design"],"stem":"An online course consists entirely of recorded videos plus end-of-module multiple-choice quizzes. Which Moore interactions are present?","options":["All three interaction types are present.","Only learner-content interaction; learner-instructor and learner-learner interactions are missing or incidental.","Only learner-instructor interaction.","Only learner-learner interaction."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Recorded videos plus auto-graded quizzes are pure learner-content. Strong online courses deliberately add the other two interaction types.","misconception_id":"video_plus_quiz_is_complete"},{"id":"online_coi_phases","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cognitive_presence","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cognitive_presence","community_of_inquiry"],"stem":"A discussion forum produces lots of messages but the conversation never moves past sharing examples and articles. Which phase of cognitive presence is the course failing to reach?","options":["Triggering event.","Exploration.","Integration and resolution; the discourse is stuck at exploration and never synthesizes or commits to a defensible answer.","All four phases are equally present."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The four-phase cognitive presence sequence requires moving past exploration into integration and resolution. Stalling at exploration is one of the most common online-learning defects.","misconception_id":"exploration_alone_is_deep_learning"},{"id":"online_async_vs_sync","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"online_learning_design","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["online_learning_design"],"stem":"Which scenario most justifies a synchronous (real-time) session over an asynchronous one?","options":["Letting learners read a complex paper at their own pace.","A real-time discourse session where learners must respond to each other's reasoning as it unfolds, with the instructor surfacing tensions in the moment.","Recording a one-way 90-minute lecture for later viewing.","Releasing a multiple-choice quiz."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Synchronous time is most valuable for live discourse and immediate feedback that asynchronous mode cannot reproduce. One-way lectures or self-paced reading should be asynchronous.","misconception_id":"sync_is_for_lectures"},{"id":"online_teaching_presence","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"teaching_presence","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["teaching_presence","community_of_inquiry"],"stem":"Teaching presence in the Community of Inquiry framework is best described as:","options":["The instructor showing their face on camera.","The design and direction of the educational experience: course architecture, facilitation of discourse, and direct instruction.","Posting more content than learners can read.","Being available 24/7 to answer questions."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Teaching presence is the design-and-facilitation function. Camera presence is one expression of it but not the construct.","misconception_id":"teaching_presence_is_camera_time"}]}}